    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191067": {
      "title": "Apply - Interfolio",
      "description": "Apply - Interfolio undefined page loaded Skip to Main Content Already have an account? Sign In Electronic Resources & Instruction Librarian Arizona State University: Office of the University Provost: ASU Library Location Phoenix, Arizona Open Date Aug 10, 2026 Description The ASU Library seeks an innovative, collaborative, and adaptable Electronic Resources & Instruction Librarian (Assistant or Associate Librarian) to support the newly established John Shufeldt School of Medicine and Medical Engineering Library at the Downtown Phoenix campus. Operating within the robust, resource-rich ecosystem of the broader ASU Library, this position will connect digital collections infrastructure to medical education. Reporting to the Director of the Medical Library, the successful candidate will leverage a deep understanding of electronic resource lifecycles, biomedical databases, and discovery systems to directly enhance student learning. The incumbent will translate their technical expertise into impactful instruction, teaching cohorts of physician-engineers how to navigate, evaluate, and master complex clinical and engineering informatics tools. This role is ideal for a professional who views seamless digital access as the vital first step toward exceptional health literacy and evidence-based medical education. Evidence of experience going beyond the traditional parameters of librarianship is highly encouraged at America's most innovative university. The successful candidate will also play a key role in supporting the school's Liaison Committee on Medical Education (LCME) accreditation process, ensuring that digital library resources, access mechanisms, and educational services demonstrably meet national medical education standards. As an Academic Professional, the incumbent is expected to maintain an active program of professional development, scholarship, and service aligned with ASU Library culture. Library Statement: The ASU Library is a center of learning, exploration and discovery. With nine locations, the library plays a vital role in expanding knowledge, cultivating community and inspiring innovation at ASU and in our communities. Students and faculty can access millions of information resources, world-class collections, outstanding study spaces, advanced data centers, and creative maker services. As supporters of student success, the library is a critical partner in realizing the New American University’s mission of access, excellence and impact. ASU Library empowers people to unlock their potential, express themselves, and learn to thrive. For more information or to find library locations, visit lib.asu.edu . About the John Shufeldt School of Medicine and Medical Engineering: The John Shufeldt School of Medicine and Medical Engineering represents a revolutionary leap forward in medical education. As one of the nation's newest medical schools, it is intentionally designed to merge clinical medicine with advanced engineering, technology, and data science. Our unique curriculum trains a new generation of \"physician-engineers\" who are equipped to both practice medicine and to actively invent solutions to complex healthcare challenges. Located in the heart of the vibrant Downtown Phoenix biomedical corridor, the John Shufeldt School of Medicine and Medical Engineering offers an intimate, highly collaborative ecosystem where cutting-edge clinical training meets immersive research and technological innovation from day one. This library position will also provide unique opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration across the downtown campus, including partnerships with the Edson College of Nursing and Health Innovation, the College of Health Solutions, and the School of Technology for Public Health. Essential Functions: o Leads the local operational lifecycle—including the evaluation, implementation, assessment, renewal, and ongoing management—of electronic resources tailored to medicine and medical engineering within the university library's discovery ecosystem. o Works in close coordination with the central ASU Library collection development and licensing teams to navigate systemic procurement workflows, vendor contracts, and university IT/security reviews for incoming digital assets. o Connects backend database functionality with classroom learning by teaching medical and medical engineering students how to effectively navigate and search complex clinical and technical databases within their regular coursework. o Partners with the Medical Library Director and library staff to design and deliver workshops, orientations, and curriculum-embedded sessions that teach advanced search mechanics and clinical tool navigation (e.g., PubMed/Medline, Scopus, IEEE Xplore). o Ensures instructional initiatives actively foster medical students' competencies in lifelong learning and self-directed investigation in alignment with LCME standards. o Develops dynamic asynchronous learning materials, anatomy platforms, training videos, online tutorials, and customized point-of-need research guides (e.g., LibGuides). o Provides specialized research consultations, citation assistance, and support for in-depth literature reviews. o Coordinates access, troubleshooting, and publisher relations for clinical databases, e-journals, and digital tools to guarantee seamless, equitable, and secure off-campus resource access. o Collects, monitors, and analyzes database usage metrics (such as COUNTER data) to inform strategic collection development, alternative access models, and library metrics required for the LCME Data Collection Instrument (DCI). o Partners in the daily operational success of the new library site by designing innovative, non-traditional reference workflows and on-demand information support pathways for a digital-first environment. o Participates in library-wide initiatives, task forces, and campus or university committees as requested. Qualifications Required Qualifications: Master's degree in library and/or information science from an American Library Association accredited program by the date of hire. Demonstrated knowledge of electronic resource management practices, discovery systems, or academic library procurement processes. Experience or strong demonstrated aptitude for classroom teaching, training, or public speaking. Preferred Qualifications: Experience working within an academic health sciences, medical school, or life sciences library environment. Hands-on experience with an Enterprise Library Services Platform, particularly Ex Libris Alma/Primo. Familiarity with medical, clinical, and engineering databases and platforms (e.g., PubMed/Medline, Scopus, IEEE Xplore). Familiarity with medical school accreditation processes (e.g., LCME) or experience contributing data to an institutional self-study or   Data Collection Instrument (DCI). Experience in collaborative projects or initiatives that integrate medicine or health sciences with technology or engineering. Demonstrated ability to translate technical system expertise, data literacy, or database architecture into clear, user-focused instruction or support. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to thrive and problem-solve in an evolving, collaborative educational environment. Salary and Rank: This is a Continuing Appointment track Academic Professional position; Assistant/Associate Librarian rank and salary dependent upon experience. Application Instructions This is a paperless search; only electronic materials will be accepted. To apply, please submit electronically to https://apply.interfolio.com/191067 the following:  A cover letter, comprehensive vita or resume, and names, addresses, phone numbers and email addresses of 3 recent professional references. No paper applications, faxes or emails will be accepted. The applicant’s last name should appear in each uploaded file name. Questions about the position should be directed to the search committee chair at: Bethany.McGowan@asu.edu.  Application deadline is October 7, 2026. Applications will continue to be accepted on a rolling basis for a reserve pool.   Applications in the reserve pool may then be reviewed in the order in which they were received until position is filled. Other Information: Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. For more information regarding ASU, visit our website: www.asu.edu . For more information about the Phoenix metropolitan area, please visit www.visitarizona.com . Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct\n            this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier\n            account and can send all application materials, including\n            confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Employment Opportunity Statement A background check is required for employment. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, protected veteran status, or any other basis protected by law. See https://www.asu.edu/aad/manuals/acd/acd401.html and https://www.asu.edu/titleIX/ . In compliance with federal law, ASU prepares an annual report on campus security and fire safety programs and resources.  ASU’s Annual Security and Fire Safety Report is available online at https://www.asu.edu/police/PDFs/ASU-Clery-Report.pdf You may request a hard copy of the report by contacting the ASU Police Department at 480-965-3456. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. or its licensors and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. Support | Careers | Accessibility Policy | Cookie settings | Privacy Policy | Terms of Service We use cookies that are necessary to make our site work. We may also use additional cookies to analyze, improve, and personalize our content and your digital experience. You can manage your cookie preferences using the Cookie settings link. For more information, see our Cookie Policy Cookie settings Accept all cookies Opt-Out Request Honored Cookie Preference Center We use cookies which are necessary to make our site work. We may also use additional cookies to analyse, improve and personalise our content and your digital experience. For more information, see our Cookie Policy and the list of Google Ad-Tech Vendors . You may choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types may impact your experience of our site and the services we are able to offer. See the different category headings below to find out more or change your settings. You may also be able to exercise your privacy choices as described in our Privacy Policy Allow all Manage Consent Preferences Strictly Necessary Cookies Always active These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. Cookie Details List‎ Functional Cookies Functional Cookies These cookies enable the website to provide enhanced functionality and personalisation. They may be set by us or by third party providers whose services we have added to our pages. If you do not allow these cookies then some or all of these services may not function properly. Cookie Details List‎ Performance Cookies Performance Cookies These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site. They help us to know which pages are the most and least popular and see how visitors move around the site. Cookie Details List‎ Targeting Cookies Targeting Cookies These cookies may be set through our site by our advertising partners.